// Lint waivers for the generated Verilog, read by Verilator.
//
// Used by tests/lint.rs, which runs `verilator --lint-only -Wall` over every
// example that DDL emits. The point of that gate is regressions: the output is
// clean today, so any warning it does not waive here is something the compiler
// started doing and did not used to.
//
// Every waiver below is a property of GENERATED code rather than a defect
// being tolerated. A rule waived for convenience would defeat the gate, so
// each one says why it cannot apply.
`verilator_config

// One `.v` holds every module the source file declared, and DDL flattens: a
// `fun` is inlined at its call site, so several modules in one file commonly
// instantiate none of each other and are all tops. examples/tagged.v has three.
// Naming one with `--top-module` would leave the others unlinted, which is the
// opposite of what this gate is for.
lint_off -rule MULTITOP

// An `extern` is by definition a module DDL did not compile -- the Verilog is
// hand-written and lives outside the program. examples/fanout.v instantiates
// `sink_ext`, and no file Verilator can be given will contain it.
lint_off -rule MODMISSING

// The module name is the DDL declaration's name and the file name is whatever
// the build was asked to write. `ddl build a.ddl -o b.v` is a normal thing to
// run, so these disagree by design.
lint_off -rule DECLFILENAME

// A pipe carries its payload type, and a consumer that reads one field of it
// leaves the rest unread -- `reg_port`'s `cmd_item[7:1]` is a 1-bit command in
// an 8-bit item. That is a fact about the DDL program, not about the lowering,
// and the lowering has no business narrowing a declared type behind the
// author's back.
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL
